/** Translation maps for siman 044 (kidney treifot) — imported by _patch-siman-044.mjs */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `The laws of kidney treifot. In it are 10 seifim. If the kidneys were removed — kosher; therefore if it was created with one kulya or with three kidneys — kosher; and likewise if the kulya was punctured or cut — even until the place of the charitz — kosher.`,
    '2#main': `If the kulya was smitten (laketah) — and this means its flesh becomes like flesh of the dead that reeks after days, that if one holds part of it, it dissolves and falls — and this illness reached the loven within the kulya — behold it is treifah. {Rama: (A sword-blow — its law is like laketah of the kulya) (Hagahot Ashiri and Mordechai; and see s.k. 6)} And likewise if putrefied material was found in the kulya even though it is not rotting, or murky or rotting water was found in it — behold it is treifah — and this is when they reach the loven; but if clear water was found in it — kosher, even if they reached the loven in it {Rama: (And even if clear water was found in a small pouch in the kulya — kosher) (Mordechai Piskei Avodah Zarah and Hagahot Ashiri and Kol Bo and Orach Chaim general rule 93)} And if blood was found in it — its law is like water; and if it was found full of water whose appearance is bad but they were not thick and murky, rather their appearance is like saffron like the appearance of honey — kosher; and there is one who forbids {Rama: (and such is the custom):}`,
    '3#main': `If the affliction was not except in the loven — even though all the rest of the kulya is healthy — treifah.`,
    '4#main': `If stones were found in the kulya — kosher.`,
    '5#main': `A kulya that shrank — treifah; and how much may it shrink and become treifah? In a thin animal until a bean (ful) — and a bean is included in treifah; and in a large animal until a medium grape — and a grape is included in treifah; and specifically when it shrank on account of illness; but if it was small from its birth — kosher. And how do we know whether it is on account of illness or not? If we see that its membrane shriveled — it is known that it is on account of illness; and if it is not shriveled but made according to the measure of a kulya — the matter is proven that from the beginning of its creation it was thus — and kosher.`,
    '6#main': `There is one who wrote that when they said "if the kidneys were removed — kosher," they meant when removed by hand or when created thus; but if removed on account of illness — since it shrank to less than the measure it was already forbidden — and how can it return to kosher status when they finished consuming and melting? And according to this one must check: if their pouch is full of recognizable milk, this is the beginning of its creation and kosher; and if a cavity and recognizable empty place is found there, it had kidneys but they melted — and treifah.`,
    '7#main': `If there were three kidneys or more and the excess ones shrank and two remained in their normal state that did not shrink — kosher.`,
    '8#main': `There is one who wrote that the loven in a kulya is laketah, and any change of appearance disqualifies it.`,
    '9#main': `A kulya that reddened entirely or in part — kosher.`,
    '10#main': `There are no treifot in the kulya of fowl.`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#_': `Until. And Shach wrote that "until" is inclusive {Rama: (and likewise if it was deficient — kosher, even if the charitz itself was punctured or cut or deficient — kosher; for only regarding laketah do we say if it reached the place of the charitz it is treifah because it pains more, but not in cut or puncture — Bach, see there)} And Orach Chaim wrote: specifically by needle; but by illness it is no worse than dissolving — treifah; and Bach wrote that the implication of the poskim who wrote stam kosher implies there is no distinction; and for practical law one must settle the matter. And not in the place of the loven — even in dissolving — kosher. And one must say: what is different here that it was done by needle — behold a needle found in the body cavity is treifah; therefore it deals when done before us and we saw the inner organs were not punctured.`,
    '2#א': `The loven. Shach wrote it is proven in the Talmud and several poskim that we require it reach inside the loven — see there. And even if the dissolving began from inside — treifah, since the essence of laketah is only in the place of the charitz; if so when the blow reaches the loven it is a deficiency from outside, for the loven under the loins enters inside the kulya and afterward exits from the kidneys outside and is exposed — Maharshal.`,
    '2#ב': `Sword. Taz wrote that a sword-blow means it split in two; and Shach disagrees and wrote that a sword-blow is different from being cut — a sword-blow is with force, namely laketah of the kulya; but cut with a calm cut — kosher.`,
    '2#ג': `That they reach. Shach explained: namely on the loven. And in clear water — even from the side of the loven — kosher; however this is specifically per the Mechaber's view; but many poskim hold that if laketah occurred even in one kulya in murky water or its flesh became like flesh of the dead — even if they did not reach the place of the charitz — treifah; and it implies even in a small amount of putrefaction far from the place of the charitz — treifah {Rama: (for the Gemara says laketah in the kulya is treifah, and it is when the affliction reaches until the place of the charitz — implying only in laketah but not in putrefaction and murky water; and see Taz and Pri Chadash who disagree and rule like Shulchan Aruch that putrefaction must reach the loven in it. An incident in an animal: putrefaction was found in the kulya that was rotting and they did not manage to examine whether it reached the place of the charitz before a dog ate it — Maharil, Rashdam, Maharshach, and Shai declared treifah. See Kenesset HaGedolah daf 161: many people were in the slaughterhouse; some said they saw the kulya and did not see putrefaction, and some said they saw putrefaction — there is no contradiction and treifah. Two testified they saw the kulya and there was no putrefaction, and two say there was putrefaction but they did not know if it reached the charitz — this is contradiction and two against two, and the animal stands in presumption of permission. They saw water in the kulya and smelled a rotting odor — do not hang and say perhaps the rot came from elsewhere and the water in the kulya are clear — Kenesset HaGedolah in name of Mahari Azulai, see there daf 161)} And so Maharshal and Bach; and Taz wrote that so one should rule for practical law; and Shach wrote: but certainly in a sword-blow all agree we require the affliction reach the place of the charitz, unlike Bach who forbids even in that; and Bach is further stringent in several laws in this siman that are not essential in Talmud and poskim. And Maharshal wrote: although the Gemara says full of putrefaction is treifah — one may distinguish: if the blister was not from outside but from inside, the putrefaction must reach the place of the charitz; therefore it said "full"; but where the blister protrudes outside and there is putrefaction — treifah even if it did not reach the charitz — end of his words.`,
    '4#_': `Stones. And Taz and Shach wrote: even if found in the place of the charitz — kosher {Rama: (for from there they descend to the ligament of the penis)} {Rama: (Turei Chadashim)}.`,
    '5#_': `Like a grape. Taz wrote: do not challenge — behold a grape is not as large as a bean; Maharshal already answered that it deals with fruits of the seven species praised in Eretz Yisrael that were exceptional in size according to nature, as we find regarding olive measure that it is half an egg of a hen though not found thus now. And Shach wrote in name of Kol Bo: if it is entirely full and there is no cavity around it and no hollowness at all — then it is recognizable that such was the beginning of its creation and kosher; and if a cavity is found there, it truly shrank and is forbidden — end {Rama: (A kulya that was small and they did not manage to measure whether it was more than a bean or grape until the animal was lost — permitted from double doubt: doubt that it was more than a bean, and doubt that perhaps from the beginning of creation it was small and not on account of illness. However, if the other kulya was rotting or any flaw — although it is not treifah from this, there is only one doubt that perhaps it was larger than a bean; but doubt that perhaps from the beginning of creation it was thus small — one cannot say, since we saw the other kulya rotting from illness we are witnesses that this kulya too shrank from illness — Kenesset HaGedolah daf 169)}.`,
    '6#_': `And treifah. And Maharshal disagrees on this and rules that even by illness it returns to its original permission {Rama: (for we find a lung that was punctured and the wall sealed it — kosher even though before the sealing it was treifah)}; but Taz and Shach rule like the Mechaber {Rama: (If one kulya was very very large — Shai ruled with great halachic authorities to permit that from the beginning of creation it was thus — and we relied on them)}.`,
    '7#_': `Two. Shach wrote in name of Bach: the wording implies the third does not stand in one row with the two but is found elsewhere; but if they stand one next to the other and there is affliction in one — treifah, for who says the permitted one was afflicted — perhaps the one that is not excess was afflicted, and it is laketah in one kulya and treifah {Rama: (and Tzitz Eliezer followed him siman 98)} {Rama: (And Taz wrote: I do not know who introduced this to add treifot through this distinction — for certainly on each there is an excess, and whenever two remain as required we say they are the kidneys — and this is simple — end; and Maharshal wrote: if there are three kidneys and all stand in one row and there is affliction or other deficiency in one of them — kosher; but if the third does not stand in the row and the affliction or deficiency is in one of those two standing in the row — treifah; and requires study for practical law; and Shach left this matter in requires study. And Pri Chadash wrote: even if an extra kulya is found, whenever there is affliction in one of them — treifah — see there)}.`,
    '8#א': `That the loven. Maharshal wrote: although it disqualifies only until affliction reaches the loven in the kulya, which is the place of the charitz — how is it recognizable that it was afflicted, behold it is already white? One may say: since it cannot stand upon it — treifah, for it is a Torah-level doubt — end; and Bach brings it.`,
    '8#ב': `Appearance. Maharshal wrote: since change of appearance in the kulya is not from the Talmud, therefore one forbids only when it reaches the loven; and Taz disagrees and holds that change of appearance even if it does not reach the loven is treifah, for in the end it will ruin everything; and it is in Tur: if it is green — kosher; and poskim disagreed whether this is what they call in foreign tongue "bleu" or "jaune"; and Shach wrote: in siman 188 we hold that appearance of "jaune" does not lean to redness even for leniency, and all the more so for stringency; and likewise above siman 38, a lung that reddened — behold there is a distinction between red and "jaune"; and so Taz: one may not permit appearance of "jaune," and in appearance of "gris" one may permit — see there {Rama: (Pri Chadash wrote: on the loven in the kulya there is a thin membrane above, and sometimes that membrane is found black leaning to greenish, and beneath that membrane they extract the loven white like other kidneys — kosher — see there. Turei Chadashim wrote: appearance of "jaune" is kosher for it leans to redness, and "gris" and "bleu" are treifah — end. And Taz and Shach disagreed and ruled that appearance of "jaune" is treifah and appearance of "bleu" is kosher; and Pri Chadash declares treifah in every case — see there)}.`,
    '10#_': `Fowl. And he wrote {Rama: (Beit Yosef in name of Orach Chaim)} that Rosh permitted even if it is swollen and black as ink {Rama: (because fowl kidneys are enclosed in bone and intestinal contents do not reach there — specifically in fowl; but in animal and wild beast there is no distinction between thin and large, and their laws are equal — as stated daf 146)}.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Mishnah Chullin daf 54.`,
    '1#ב': `Rif and Rosh there from the Baraisa "and the two kidneys," etc. — Bechorot daf 39.`,
    '2#א': `Rambam's language in chapter 8 of Hilchot Shechitah; and so is the Rif's version, and so Rashba and Rosh and Shulchan Aruch.`,
    '2#ב': `Tur; and so Rashba in his responsum.`,
    '2#ג': `Hagahot Ashiri in name of Bahag and Orach Chaim general rule 53 and Kol Bo.`,
    '2#ד': `Beit Yosef from the words of Hagahot Ashiri.`,
    '2#ה': `Responsum of Rashba.`,
    '3#_': `Beit Yosef from implication of the Gemara and as the law of liver that yellowed in an olive-volume in the place of gall and an olive-volume in the place where it is alive.`,
    '4#_': `Rokeach and Orach Chaim in name of an incident, etc.`,
    '5#א': `Conclusion of the Gemara there daf 55.`,
    '5#ב': `Rosh there and Rashba in his responsum and Shulchan Aruch; and so is implied from Rashi's words.`,
    '5#ג': `Ran's language there.`,
    '6#_': `Kol Bo according to Raavad's view.`,
    '7#_': `Hagahot Ashiri.`,
    '8#_': `Tur in name of Be'er HaEsh.`,
    '9#_': `Rokeach.`,
    '10#_': `Tur in name of Rashba and in name of great teachers; and he is Rambam in chapter 10 of Hilchot Shechitah law 10; and Agur in name of Maharil.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#_': `And likewise if cut, etc. As stated there 55b that puncture is kosher in the kulya; and therefore "until the place of the charitz" — for otherwise even laketah would be kosher as stated: (Likut) And likewise if punctured, etc. As stated; and all the more so per Rif's version and Shulchan Aruch there is disqualification, etc. — it implies the place of affliction disqualifies; and so Rashi from Megillah 1, Rosh HaShanah to exclude, etc.; and so Rosh and Shulchan Aruch; and he wrote from what is stated that he struck the kulya, etc., and he did not examine whether it reached the charitz; and Hagahot Ashiri 36, entry "and now," etc. (end).`,
    '2#א': `And this means its flesh becomes, etc. As the Rif's version: kosher, etc.; putrefaction disqualifies in the kulya, etc.; laketah disqualifies in this, etc.; and therefore this is not putrefaction.`,
    '2#ב': `Until the loven. Meaning: inside the loven — end.`,
    '2#ג': `Sword-blow, etc. As stated Megillah 1: "He splits my kidneys" — who lives? — not like Tosafot's explanation there; and in Sha'arim he explains laketah from sword-blow.`,
    '2#ד': `And likewise if found, etc. This is putrefaction mentioned there; and there Megillah 48a.`,
    '2#ה': `Or if found, etc. There they did not say, etc.; and these are four distinctions: laketah disqualifies in both; clear water is kosher in both; puncture and putrefaction — what disqualifies in one is kosher in the other.`,
    '2#ו': `But if, etc. And even. Gemara there; and therefore it says "when they reach," for otherwise all would be kosher as above.`,
    '2#ז': `And if, etc. All this is when they reach the place of the charitz; and they disagreed whether their law is like putrefaction or murky, or their law is like clear water.`,
    '2#ח': `And if found, etc. So Beit Yosef learned from blood; but Darkei Moshe wrote blood is different, as Orach Chaim wrote this is not laketah for it is entirely blood; therefore the main view is Rashba. There.`,
    '3#_': `If not, etc. So Beit Yosef learned from Rashba's words in siman 52 seif 1: "or if it yellowed," etc.; and so in siman 41 seif 63 in the hagahah "and if they dried," etc.; and proof for their words from what is stated 46a: he said to him regarding this excess, if, etc. — it implies specifically that these two olive-volumes remained.`,
    '4#_': `Found, etc. End of Shach.`,
    '5#א': `And a bean is included, etc. And a grape is included, etc. As stated 1: all measures, etc.`,
    '5#ב': `And specifically, etc. And this is what it means "that it shrank."`,
    '6#_': `And how, etc. Perforce why treifah since, etc.`,
    '8#_': `There is one who wrote, etc. All this is included in what is stated laketah, etc.`,
    '10#_': `(Likut) There are no, etc. As stated above siman 43 seif 6 regarding spleen; and further that there is no loven in fowl and treifot are only in the loven. Responsum of Rashba 2; and see there that he is uncertain about this (end).`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `A) [Seif 1] If the kidneys were removed — kosher. And likewise if one was removed — kosher. Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Tur ot 1; Perishah ot 1; Shaarei Tzedek ot 1; Anaf Yosef ot 1; Zivchei Tzedek ot 1; and this is if removed by hand or created thus; but if removed on account of illness — see below seif 6.`,
    '2#_': `B) There. Therefore if created with one kulya, etc. And likewise if created without kidneys at all — kosher. Chagurat Shmuel. And so Rashbatz in Yavin Shemuah daf 13: there are creatures lacking kidneys; therefore "kidneys removed" is kosher. Machatzit HaShekel ot 3; Anaf Yosef there; Zivchei Tzedek ot 2.`,
    '3#_': `C) There. Or with three kidneys — kosher. For we say every excess is considered as removed; and a Baraisa also teaches thus: "and the two kidneys" — not one who has one kulya and not one who has three; and from the fact the Merciful One disqualifies for the Most High, we infer for ordinary use it is permitted. Beit Yosef in name of Rif and Rosh. And so Mordechai Piskei Avodah Zarah. Levush. Mishnat Zekenim ot 1; Zivchei Tzedek ot 2.`,
    '4#_': `D) There. Or with three kidneys — kosher. And likewise four, five, and more. Machatzit HaShekel ot 4 in name of several poskim; Anaf Yosef there; Zivchei Tzedek ot 3; and so below seif 7.`,
    '5#_': `E) And even if the three kidneys adhere to the charitz — kosher for all, even per Ramban. Bach. Pri Chadash ot 1; Lev HaPanim ot 1; Beit Lechem Yehudah ot 1; Machatzit HaShekel ot 5; Anaf Yosef there; Beit Yitzchak in Sha'ar 7 ot 2; Zivchei Tzedek there ot 4.`,
    '6#_': `F) And although if they adhere on account of illness at the place of the charitz and it is possible to know they adhere from illness — one may declare kosher even without double doubt. Machatzit HaShekel ot 6; Anaf Yosef there ot 3; Zivchei Tzedek ot 5.`,
    '7#_': `G) And if the three kidneys adhere to one another such that if the place of adhesion is removed no measure of kulya remains — until a bean in a thin animal and like a grape in a large one (as below seif 5) — one should declare treifah without double doubt. Mishnat Zekenim ot 1; Machatzit HaShekel ot 7; Anaf Yosef there ot 2; Beit Yitzchak in Sha'ar 7; Zivchei Tzedek there ot 6.`,
    '8#_': `H) There. And likewise if the kulya was punctured or cut — even until the place of the charitz — kosher. And the place of the charitz means where the sinews are entwined there, and it is the middle of the kulya. Rashi Chullin 55b. And in Rashba's responsum it is explained more: the place of the charitz is the loven under the loins that enters inside the kulya. Beit Yosef. Levush.`,
    '9#_': `I) There. Even until the place of the charitz — kosher. "Until" is inclusive — even if it reached the loven itself — kosher in puncture or cut. Bach. Taz s.k. 3. Shach s.k. 4. Pri Chadash ot 3; Lev HaPanim ot 5; Mishnat Zekenim ot 3 and in Sha'ar 4 ot 4; Shaarei Tzedek ot 5; Turei Zahav siman 66 ot 4; Beit Yitzchak in Sha'ar 7 ot 5.`,
    '10#_': `J) If it was cut and there is no deficiency — even until the place of the charitz — kosher. Machatzit HaShekel ot 9; Zivchei Tzedek ot 7.`,
  },
  kereti: {
    '1#א': `Cut — see Peleti who explained that it was cut in thickness and not across, only part of the thickness was cut.`,
    '1#ב': `Even per Tur's view that in deficiency even if the deficiency reached the charitz — kosher; and Maharshal challenged; and Taz strengthened Tur's words; and see Peleti where I brought support and proof for both views — therefore one should be stringent; and likewise puncture by illness — Ra'ah's view is to be stringent; and for us who are not expert in dissolving — it is obvious treifah.`,
    '1#ג': `Until the place, etc. And internal deficiency — Maharshal's view is to permit only almost, which is not applicable, for affliction must reach the place of the charitz where it is exposed; and I did not understand that kulya flesh and fat outside can be fine while inside is bad full of putrefaction and laketah — see Peleti where I wrote proof they call deficiency in the kulya.`,
    '1#ד': `Place of the charitz; and if the loven itself was punctured — later authorities [permit].`,
    '1#ה': `Charitz is the loven, for there is fat; and "until" is inclusive — so is the view of later authorities. And see Peleti: one may say if affliction reached the loven somewhat, even if not majority of the loven — treifah; unlike if there is only laketah in the loven — must be majority of the loven. Also if we saw laketah and did not manage to examine the loven until it was lost — treifah; but if there is only laketah in the loven — must know for certain, and if not — not kosher.`,
    '2#א': `Until the loven is the charitz passing through the middle of the kulya; above it is fat and below it to its right are sinews entwined in them.`,
    '2#ב': `Sword-blow — Maharshal explained it split in two; Shach challenged that this is not the intent in Shulchan Aruch; and so Peleti; and I wrote that if it split in two — even without a sword — treifah; and see what I wrote on this; and Shach wrote the explanation is that it was not cut calmly but with force — this is strained; and see Peleti where I wrote: not that it was cut, but wounds were made in the kulya by sword — this is danger, and it is written "exceedingly" as we hold regarding desecration of Shabbat — see there.`,
    '2#ג': `Until the loven — Shach's view in name of many authors: in dissolving we do not require affliction until the charitz; and see Peleti where I brought proof for Shulchan Aruch's words.`,
    '2#ד': `Blood — Maharshal is stringent; and see Peleti where I wrote: when the measure of the kulya is whole and only the remainder melted and was blood; or only its appearance is like blood and it is not actually blood.`,
    '4#_': `Stones — even in the place of the charitz, for so is the order — Pri Chadash.`,
    '6#א': `And how can it return to kosher — see Peleti where I wrote that likewise in spleen removed by illness one may say: from the beginning it dissolved in the supports — treifah; and afterward by illness the entire thing was removed — how can it return to kosher.`,
    '6#ב': `Return — and Maharshal disagrees; and see Peleti at length.`,
    '7#א': `If there were three kulyot and they stand in one place — disqualify in one of them, lest it is the proper kulya that is not the excess; and see Peleti in siman 43 where I proved to be stringent in all kulyot.`,
    '7#ב': `Loven in the kulya — and even if not recognizable in the loven, from doubt we declare treifah as above when the loven was lost; and Peri Megadim's view is to declare treifah for any change of appearance.`,
    '8#_': `Three kidneys — see Peleti where I elaborated whether it exists in reality to be created with only one kulya; and I wrote it is a dispute of Rambam and Tosafot.`,
    '10#_': `Kulya of fowl — for all treifot require the charitz, and this does not apply in fowl; and see Peleti: the difficulty from shrunken kulya that the charitz must also shrink proportionally is not a difficulty; but if in length it remained and only in width it shrank and there is no laketah to the place of the charitz — one may say kosher; requires study; see Peleti resolving Agur.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 44, Taz s.k. 8) Clear water permits. This kal vachomer is a challenge, for putrefaction proves: kosher in lung, disqualifies in kulya.`,
    '2#_': `(Shach s.k. 16) Lest the kulya that is not the excess. Nevertheless if putrefaction was found and did not reach the place of the charitz — one may say it is a double doubt: doubt lest it is the excess; doubt that putrefaction too is not treifah without reaching the place of the charitz.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 44, Taz s.k. 8) And one must say Rama explains, etc. — meaning as Shach s.k. 7 wrote.`,
    '2#_': `(Taz s.k. 14) And I do not know, etc. See Shach s.k. 16, proof for his words: Hagahah of the Gaon Maharatz z"l in Bava Kamma 16 wrote on Bach's law and requires study for practical law; and in Taz s.k. 14 he wrote he does not know who introduced this to add treifot, etc.; and the Gaon Maharatz z"l wrote above in Shach s.k. 16, proof for Taz's words — it appears he agrees with Taz; and in my humble opinion there is clear proof for Bach's words from siman 35 regarding two roses where one is inverted and the other not — we follow the larger; and the Gaon Maharatz z"l wrote in s.k. 13 that if both are equal — treifah; and he brought this in name of several poskim; and there too why not say the non-inverted is the rose and the non-inverted has the character of a rose and the inverted is like a small rose; rather certainly since both are doubtful we say perhaps the inverted is the rose and treifah — so too here. And also from what the Gaon Maharatz z"l wrote in s.k. 17, the reason is treifah from Torah-level doubt — so too here. Thus far.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 44 seif 2) If blood was found in it — its law is like water. And per Shach's view it must be that even if the loven itself changed to blood it returns to kosher status, and if it changed to milk — not so; but per Atzei Tzvi one may say it deals when laketah reached the loven and there it is red and blood was found. And Baal Shevuyim challenges from what is stated above regarding liver in siman 41 that melted liver is treifah, and what is stated here regarding kulya that it is kosher; and the resolution: there it deals when the entire liver melted, i.e. the whole liver like this — certainly it does not return to kosher; not so here, it deals when the kulya is whole on all sides and only inside the flesh is full of blood — in this it certainly returns to health.`,
    '2#_': `(Shulchan Aruch seif 8) There is one who wrote that the loven in the kulya, etc. And they challenge per Shach and other acharonim who hold specifically this change of appearance must be on the loven — why disqualify in the loven, behold there is no change of appearance at all? And they answer: since according to the loven it is recognizable that there is affliction there, presumably the loven itself was also afflicted and treifah; and accordingly one may answer what they challenge above per Shach's view: it is not treifah until affliction reaches the loven, and if only the loven was afflicted it is also treifah — why did the Gemara need to say "and it is when the affliction reaches the place of the charitz" — for in the rest of the kulya there is none, only in the loven — it should have said only "if laketah in the loven — treifah." And what resolves the difficulty: here it deals in every case where there is laketah in any change of appearance that disqualifies — even if the kulyot changed to white; if so, if not for laketah also in the flesh of the kulya there is no possibility of this laketah in the loven at all; therefore it states laketah in the kulya — meaning the flesh was also afflicted; and if the flesh changed to white appearance, presumably affliction reached the loven and it is treifah.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 44 in Shulchan Aruch seif 2) And if blood was found in it — its law is like water. Turei Chadashim general rule 9 wrote: not specifically blood in the kulya, but likewise water that has the color of blood is not called murky unless the appearance of the water changed to another color. And so I ruled several times for practical law.`,
    '2#_': `(There seif 5) A kulya that shrank, etc. Shach agreed in his long book that in great financial loss, in such a case one expert in estimating may declare kosher between whether a bean is included or not, per Bahag and Tosafot who wrote there is no need to be stringent except in Mishnah or Baraisa. However it is difficult, for even in an amora's statement we inquire in Piskei Avodah Zarah and it was not resolved. And similarly Mahadeni challenged in Berakhot chapter 2; and I wrote there to resolve — but here it cannot be resolved. However what Sefer Etz Chaim brought as proof to forbid from Yerushalmi Sukkah regarding "etrog until like a nut — like the nut itself" — there it is a Baraisa and we hold measures to be stringent; not so here, for it is not a Baraisa.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `Or with three kidneys, etc. For every excess is considered as removed.`,
    '1#ב': `Punctured or cut. The reason in Beit Yosef in name of Rosh: only regarding laketah in seif 2 is it treifah, for it pains more than the pain of laketah in dissolving; puncture or cut — and in Tur it is written punctured or deficient even if the deficiency reached, etc.; and Maharshal wrote on this and it is not clear, for we do not declare treifah in dissolving except that it is considered as if it is not; if so, all the more so deficiency — treifah if the deficiency reached the charitz; and this proof is not worth even to be stringent against Tur's view, for certainly dissolving is worse than deficiency, for in dissolving the pain grows and ruins even the place of the charitz, unlike in deficiency.`,
    '1#ג': `Until the place of the charitz. Meaning: white fat under the loins that enters inside the kulya where sinews are entwined, and it is the middle of the kulya and is called charitz. Rashi and Ran. And even if the loven itself was punctured or cut — kosher as above; and proof from what we say in the Gemara Avodah Zarah: disqualifies in lung, kosher in kulya — meaning puncture; from which we learn in the entire kulya it is kosher; and further from what Rosh taught that punctured or cut is kosher from what we say in the Gemara they struck the kulya and it was not treifah and they did not examine whether it reached the loven — we learn even if it reached the loven itself it is kosher in puncture or cut; and so Moharach z"l.`,
    '2#א': `And this means its flesh becomes, etc. I already wrote the reason in seif 1 that this pains most. And Maharshal wrote: at first glance it appears we require the dissolving begin also from outside, for otherwise it is not worse than lung, and we do not find laketah in kulya should be worse than lung; and all the more so it is like lung that internal deficiency is not called deficiency; but it is not so, since the essence of laketah is only in the place of the charitz; if so when the blow reaches the loven of the kulya this is not internal deficiency but external, for the loven under the loins enters inside the kulya and afterward exits from the kidneys outside and is exposed; therefore it implies from this that regarding spleen we say properly it is kosher when afflicted from inside; and I already wrote from this end of siman 43.`,
    '2#ב': `Sword-blow. At first glance it is astonishing, for this means cut, and this litigant is Hagahot Ashiri, and it is part of cut too, and he holds treifah — for only in puncture is it kosher; and Maharshal wrote on this and it is not clear — from where do we divide between puncture and cut, for certainly wherever kosher in puncture, kosher in cut per Rosh's view; nevertheless if cut in two or split — Raavyah ruled treifah; if so Rama should have brought "there is one who says if cut — treifah"; and one must say Rama explains this sword-blow means split in two specifically, per Raavyah whom Hagahot Ashiri brings.`,
    '2#ג': `And it is when they reach the loven. This is Rashba's and Tur's view; and it depends on variant readings in the Gemara: Rashi reads kosher in lung, disqualifies in kulya — meaning putrefaction only; and Alfasi reads disqualifies in this and in this — laketah; and therefore laketah and putrefaction are two matters; and therefore they explained laketah means dissolving as copied here at the beginning of this seif; and it is also in the Gemara: laketah in the kulya is treifah when it reaches the loven. Now per Rashi putrefaction is laketah — automatically in putrefaction we require reaching the loven; but per Rif laketah is a different matter, and since in laketah alone we say it must reach the loven — automatically in putrefaction we do not say this, and therefore treifah even without reaching the loven; and so Beit Yosef in name of Sha'arim of R' Shmuel ben Chafni; and Maharshal wrote this is implied from Rambam's words, etc.; and so appears to be the essence, not like Rashba and Tur who wrote even in putrefaction and murky water we require reaching the place of the charitz — from where do we lenient in Torah prohibitions without proof from the Talmud — end; and so Moharach z"l; and so one should rule for practical law. And he further wrote: although we say in the Gemara full of putrefaction is treifah — apparently "full" is required and it is not treifah until reaching the place of the charitz; one may distinguish: if the blister was not from outside but from inside, the putrefaction must reach the place of the charitz; therefore it said "full"; but where the blister protrudes outside and there is putrefaction — treifah even if it did not reach the charitz; and he brings proof on this from Or Zaru'a.`,
    '2#ד': `That they reach the loven. Meaning: also in the loven there is this blow; and so at the beginning in dissolving, as Beit Yosef wrote in name of Orach Chaim that the city's sages agreed wherever it does not reach the loven itself — kosher; and Beit Yosef brought in name of Orach Chaim that affliction must be specifically in the place of the charitz; and I already wrote in name of Maharshal that in putrefaction we do not require the place of the charitz.`,
    '2#ה': `Even if they reached the loven, etc. It implies even in the loven itself — difficult from what Beit Yosef wrote in name of Hagahot Ashiri regarding a ram of Shafir'an where there was no flesh at all, only clear water, only in the place of the loven it was whole and he declared kosher — end; it implies if clear water reached the loven — treifah; and this is not so, for why be more stringent in clear water than in puncture and cut, for we proved above that kosher even in the loven itself; and here in clear water they required the entire loven be whole — behold kal vachomer: what puncture or cut that disqualifies in lung nevertheless is kosher in the loven's loven — why should clear water that is kosher in lung be forbidden in the loven even in part? Therefore it appears in that incident of Hagahot Ashiri they did not declare kosher specifically because the entire loven must be whole, but the incident was thus and not precise; rather even in part, since they were clear.`,
    '2#ו': `If blood was found in it, etc. Beit Yosef in name of Hagahot Ashiri in name of Bahag likewise; and he proved from the fact blood is not counted among murky and rotting; and Maharshal wrote: wonder if we should infer the other side stringently — only clear water is kosher and not blood; also it is not reasonable blood should be better than murky, for there is no greater laketah than if full of blood or a blister outside full of blood — end; and Orach Chaim wrote the reason for blood being kosher: this is not laketah for it is entirely blood — end; and accordingly Rashba's challenge is resolved why he did not count it among clear water — all the more so kosher in blood.`,
    '3#_': `Except in the loven. Meaning: what is mentioned above forbidding in the kulya forbids in the loven alone, for the essence of its vitality is in that loven; and it was already explained nearby that from laketah that disqualifies we require the whole be whole; and even in clear water [we require at least part remain].`,
    '4#_': `Stones found in the kulya. Meaning: and the entire place of the loven is healthy — so Beit Yosef in name of Orach Chaim; and Rokeach wrote the reason that from there they descend to the ligament of the penis; and Turei Chadashim wrote from this it implies even if stones are found in the place of the charitz — and so it appears to me — end.`,
    '5#_': `And a grape is included. Beit Yosef in name of Mahari ibn Chaviv explained what he explained here, for it was challenged: behold a grape is not as large as a bean; and Maharshal wrote this is not a wonder, for fruits of the seven species praised in Eretz Yisrael were exceptional in size according to nature, for we have received tradition from Tosafot authors that olive is half an egg of a hen though not found thus now, only they changed — end.`,
    '6#_': `And how can it return to kosher status. This is Kol Bo's view; and Maharshal disagrees and wrote: behold we find likewise a lung that was punctured and sealing in its flesh helps even though before sealing it was treifah; and only by human action do we say regarding meeting of sinews it does not return to kosher — end; he holds even if removed by illness it is kosher. And in my humble opinion, like Kol Bo: certainly where we find explicitly this matter that treifah can return to kosher status we find it; but here who will tell us they permitted this? One may say they did not intend this. [Only] if removed from birth; and from where do we say even by illness it is so, since once it was treifah? And there is no general rule for lung punctured and sealed in flesh, for there the essence of treifah is from puncture and the puncture was already sealed, unlike here. And further we hold in simanim 39 and 36 regarding lung sealing does not help, for it is initial sealing as Rashi and poskim wrote, unlike here; and so acharonim wrote. But say: since it will entirely rot, why declare treifah when it shrinks from illness — it will return healthy; do not say perhaps healing will come that it will not rot further — if so all the more so why declare treifah, for when its wound heals it will certainly return as before; rather it is simple they forbade when it shrinks, for it will waste away and entirely rot — therefore treifah; and this reasoning is certainly sound, and so one should rule like Kol Bo — so it appears to me.`,
    '7#_': `And the excess ones shrank, etc. Moharach z"l wrote the wording implies the third does not stand in one row with the two; but if they stand one next to the other and one was afflicted — treifah, for who says the permitted one was afflicted — perhaps the one that is not excess was afflicted, and it is laketah in one kulya and treifah — end of his language. And I do not know who introduced this to add treifot through this distinction — for certainly on each there is an excess, and whenever two remain as required we say they are its kidneys — and this is simple in my humble opinion.`,
    '8#_': `And any change of appearance, etc. Tur wrote if it is green — kosher; and Beit Yosef thought he learned this from Beit Yosef's words; therefore he wondered from where he got this; and it appears simple this is his own view, that this is not a general rule of change of appearance and it is not laketah at all, as we say regarding lung because blood did not fall on it, and they did not include every change of appearance among laketot; and so is the essence for halachah. And Turei Chadashim wrote appearance of "jaune" is kosher for it leans to redness, and "gris" and "bleu" are treifah — very astonishing why it should differ from lung where we say the opposite; especially in green, the reason is because blood did not fall; and so is the essence, for Tur wrote explicitly green is kosher, and there is reason to permit — namely green "gris," and this did not enter the general rule of appearance at all. And what he wrote that "jaune" leans to redness is difficult, for in siman 188 regarding menstrual blood it is not so; therefore one may not permit appearance of "jaune." And Maharshal wrote: since change of appearance in the kulya has no proof from the Talmud, therefore one forbids in change of appearance only when it reaches its loven; and it does not imply thus in Beit Yosef's words that he brought, who did not distinguish between kulya and lung except in appearances that are not equal; but in this that in lung treifah even if it did not enter inside, as we proved above siman 38 — likewise in kulya, and the reason is it will ruin everything in the end, as stated.`,
    '10#_': `In the kulya of fowl. The reason in name of Mahari Molin: because fowl kidneys are enclosed in bone and intestinal contents do not reach there; and Beit Yosef wrote in name of Orach Chaim; and for this reason Rosh permitted a hen whose kulya swelled like a hen's egg and was black as ink — end.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `If the kidneys were removed, etc. See above s.k. 15.`,
    '1#ב': `And likewise if punctured. Orach Chaim wrote: specifically by needle; but by illness it is no worse than dissolving; and Bach wrote: however the implication of poskim who wrote stam that punctured or deficient is kosher implies there is no distinction; and for practical law one must settle the matter; and not in the place of the loven — even in dissolving — kosher.`,
    '1#ג': `Or cut. See above s.k. 7.`,
    '1#ד': `Until the place of the charitz. And "until" is inclusive — kosher — so is proven in Talmud and poskim; and so Bach wrote.`,
    '2#א': `Until the loven. Proven in Talmud and several poskim that we require reaching inside the loven; and so Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe in name of Orach Chaim explicitly; and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 87; therefore one must say what Tur and Mechaber wrote "until the loven" is not precise; and so Bach that it is not precise; and so in some versions of Tur and in Perishah and Beit Yosef it was copied "on the loven," unlike the implication in Atzei Tzvi that until the loven alone is treifah.`,
    '2#ב': `The loven. That enters inside the kidneys under the loins where sinews are entwined in the middle of the kulya, and it is in the place of the charitz. Rashi.`,
    '2#ג': `Sword-blow. Bach explained this means specifically that it split in two, and it is not clear as I wrote in my book; and so is explicit in Maharshal's words Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 86 that sword-blow means even if it did not split in two; and so is implied from Orach Chaim's words that I brought in s.k. 8; however in Maharshal's words there it is explained that sword-blow means cut; and if so Taz and other poskim who wrote in seif 1 that cut is kosher do not seem to hold thus; indeed Orach Chaim after writing that in sword-blow or split in two is treifah wrote: but if only cut in them and they did not divide in two, even if the cut reached the place of the charitz — kosher — until here his language; therefore cut is not sword-blow; and this is R' Baruch's view in the hagahah; and it appears sword-blow means with force as one strikes with a sword, namely laketah of the kulya; but cut with a calm cut — kosher; and so it appears to me to reconcile the poskim; and in Lev Chaim 168b he left R' Baruch's words in requires study.`,
    '2#ד': `Until the loven. I already wrote in s.k. 5 that this means on the loven; and in clear water — even on the loven — kosher; and as stated "even if they reached the loven," unlike Atzei Tzvi who wrote that in murky or rotting water it is treifah when they reach the loven even though the loven is whole, and in clear water kosher only until the loven specifically provided the loven is whole — end — this does not exist; rather the essence of treifah depends on the loven; therefore the Mechaber wrote in seif 3 (and so Atzei Tzvi himself) that if the affliction was only in the loven alone — treifah; and all this is simple. However this is specifically per Tur, Mechaber, Rashba and their supporters; but Orach Chaim general rule 53 law 4 wrote: R' Shmuel ben Chafni the priest wrote in his Sha'arim: the animal was afflicted even only in one kulya in murky or rotting water, or its flesh became like flesh of the dead that reeks after days, that if one holds part of it, it dissolves and falls — treifah, even if it did not reach the place of the charitz, i.e. until the place of the loven — end; and Raavyah also wrote this: that requiring reaching the place of the charitz in the kulya is in sword-blow, but through putrefaction — even if it did not reach the place of the charitz — treifah; and he concludes: and likewise if split in two — treifah, from "He splits my kidneys" (Job 16:13) — end Orach Chaim (and it implies that even in a small amount of putrefaction far from the place of the charitz — treifah); and Beit Yosef brought this view of these poskim — see there Hagahot Ashiri; and so in Mordechai Piskei Avodah Zarah. And he wrote: it implies they explain what we say in the Talmud that we require the affliction reach the place of the charitz — only in sword-blow laketah; and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 86: in putrefaction even if it did not reach the loven — treifah; and so in Turei Chadashim general rule 9 law 1 he brings the forbidders at the end; but certainly in sword-blow all agree we require the affliction reach the place of the charitz (and what the rabbi wrote in the hagahah that sword-blow's law is like laketah of the kulya means it is treifah for all when it reached the place of the charitz — examine); and so Mordechai and Hagahot Ashiri in name of Rashi; and so Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 46, unlike Bach who forbids even in sword-blow if it did not reach the place of the charitz; and Bach is further stringent in several laws in this seif that are not essential in Talmud and poskim.`,
    '2#ה': `Kosher. So Beit Yosef per Hagahot Ashiri who permits blood; and Sefer Lev Chaim end of daf 168 wrote: one should wonder on Rama that he did not emend to forbid blood as he emended to forbid appearance of honey — end; and incidentally he did not examine Darkei Moshe or Turei Chadashim (and he answered that in his book Torat HaEsh he corrected this) — in Darkei Moshe he wrote on Beit Yosef: and it is not clear, for the reason for blood is as Orach Chaim wrote this is not laketah since it is entirely blood; but otherwise no (meaning: and like appearance of honey it is called murky water; and so Darkei Moshe and Turei Chadashim there explicitly, and not because of change of appearance we declare treifah, for he permits in Turei Chadashim there law 6 appearance of "jaune" that is like saffron as written in beginning of siman 38 — examine); therefore one should rule as Rashba wrote — end; and so Turei Chadashim general rule 9 law 4; and for this he wrote here in the hagahah "and such is the custom."`,
    '3#_': `Even though all the rest, etc. See above siman 41 s.k. 3.`,
    '4#_': `Stones found in the kulya — kosher. For from there they descend to the ligament of the penis — end Rokeach; and it implies even if found in the place of the charitz; and Maharshal Piskei Avodah Zarah siman 87 and Bach wrote the opposite — see there Rokeach; and I do not know from where they got this; perhaps they wanted to equate it to Orach Chaim's words; however in Turei Chadashim there law 6 he wrote: perhaps Orach Chaim also concedes in this, only he stated the incident that occurred; and so it appears to me, since the way of a stone is to grow in kidneys and it is among physicians' practices — one should not declare treifah even in the place of the charitz — end; and also the proof Maharshal brought there from Hagahot Ashiri's incident in Shafir'an is no proof, for an incident that occurred thus is not proof; nevertheless from Orach Chaim's words it implies he stated it specifically because we read "in the place of the charitz," etc. — see there; and Beit Yosef brings it.`,
    '5#א': `And a bean is included — treifah. And in Bahag it is written that bean and grape are kosher; and so Raavan in siman 257: until a bean — if the kulya is [the size of] a bean it is kosher; and in a large animal like a medium grape, which is more than a bean; and in their places in Eretz Yisrael grapes were large; and I saw an incident in Mainz in a large animal that shrank like a grape and they declared it kosher — end; and so Or Zaru'a; and Maharshal brings it in his Orach Chaim siman 88.`,
    '5#ב': `Like a grape. And Turei Chadashim there law 7 wrote; and Beit Yosef wrote in name of Mahari ibn Chaviv: it does not mean an actual grape, but the kulya of a large animal is made of somewhat separate pieces, one of which is called a grape — end; and so in Simanim. And it is astonishing, for Mahari ibn Chaviv concludes the essence is an actual grape; and so agreed to this explanation Maharshal end of siman 88, Bach, Perishah, and so Raavan whom I brought in the previous s.k.`,
    '5#ג': `And how do we know, etc. And in Kol Bo: how do we know? We examine their places: if it is entirely full and there is no cavity around it and no hollowness at all — then it is recognizable that such was the beginning of its creation and kosher; and if a cavity is found there, it truly shrank and is forbidden — thus far.`,
    '6#_': `Or when created thus, etc. So is Kol Bo; and Maharshal there challenged him: and it is not clear, for in the Mishnah it teaches "removed — kosher," and every "removed" (specific act) [should read: implies] by human beings or by illness (as below end siman 50); rather certainly it deals when removed by illness or by a person; and what he wondered — how can it return to kosher status — why should it not return by itself, since it is not in human hands, as we declare kosher a lung that was punctured and sealed in its flesh even though before sealing it was treifah — end; and Bach brings it; and his words are not plausible, for certainly Kol Bo also holds that even when the entire thing is removed as one by a person it is kosher, as the Mechaber and Derishah wrote; rather he comes to say: by illness when it shrank initially — how can it return to kosher status? And there is no general rule for lung punctured and sealed in flesh, for there the essence of treifah is from puncture and the puncture was already sealed, unlike here; and further we hold in simanim 39 and 36 regarding lung sealing does not help, for it is initial sealing as Rashi and poskim wrote, unlike here; and so acharonim wrote.`,
    '7#_': `If there were three kidneys. Bach wrote: the wording implies the third does not stand in one row with the two but is found elsewhere; but if they stand one next to the other and there is affliction in one — then it is treifah, for who says the permitted one was afflicted — perhaps the kulya that is not the excess was afflicted, and it is laketah in one kulya and treifah — end; and there is apparently proof for his words from what Raavan wrote there: when there are three kidneys and one of them shrank — treifah; but Maharshal there siman 89 wrote the opposite: if there are three kidneys and all stand in one row and there is affliction or other deficiency in one of them — kosher; but if the third does not stand in the row and the affliction or deficiency is in one of those two standing in the row — treifah — end; see there; and per his words Raavan must deal with the case where the third does not stand in the row and one of those standing in the row was afflicted; and requires study for practical law.`,
    '8#א': `That the loven in the kulya is laketah. And Maharshal wrote there end of siman 87: although it disqualifies only until affliction reaches the loven in the kulya, which is the place of the charitz — how is it recognizable that it was afflicted, behold it is already white? One may say: since it cannot stand upon it — treifah, for it is a Torah-level doubt — end; and Bach brings it.`,
    '8#ב': `And any change, etc. And Tur wrote if it is green — kosher; and so Orach Chaim end of general rule 23; and so Maharshal there, Bach; and they explained this is specifically green like a leek (called in foreign tongue "bleu," as above siman 38); and so Perishah seif 4; but in Turei Chadashim there end of law 6 he wrote: this is specifically green called "jaune" that also leans to redness; but green like a leek and such called "bleu" — treifah — end; and so in Sefer Efi Ravrevi; and requires study, for we hold beginning of siman 188 that appearance of green "jaune" does not lean to redness even for leniency, and all the more so for stringency; and likewise above siman 38, a lung that reddened is kosher and green "jaune" is treifah — behold there is a distinction between red and "jaune."`,
    '8#ג': `Disqualifies it. And although in all organs except lung change of appearance does not disqualify — the kulya is different, since laketah disqualifies in it and change of appearance is laketah, as stated in the Talmud chapter 1 of treifot (daf 47b), and as written in siman 38 in s.k. 8.`,
    '10#_': `There are no treifot, etc. Maharshal wrote there siman 89: in the kulya one may be lenient more in fowl (than in animal) because Agur wrote in name of Maharil (and Beit Yosef brings it) that fowl kidneys are enclosed in bone and intestinal contents do not reach there — end; and so is implied from Ra'ah in Sefer Beit HaBechirah daf 50; and Atzei Tzvi's words here are hard to fit them; and Lechem Chamudot 168b already challenged; and perhaps there is a scribal error in his words — see there.`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#_': `Or cut, etc. And although many authors and Maharshal hold that if split in two it is treifah, and they derive from Job "He splits my kidneys" — meaning split in two like splitting and cleaving wood; and if so, when cut until the place of the charitz it is split in two, for when we require equality to one, one must be larger than the other. It appears to me by way of parable: the kulya is a handbreadth thick and a handbreadth wide; if cut through its thickness until it split entirely through — behold they are two parts — treifah; but if not the entire thickness was cut, only in width a slice was cut but in thickness it remained whole and did not split — kosher; and this is simple, and so the matter is explained in Orach Chaim.`,
    '2#_': `Cut — Taz in s.k. 2 brought Tur who wrote: deficient even if the deficiency reached the charitz — nevertheless kosher; and Maharshal disagreed: what is stated dissolving and what is stated deficient; and Taz rejects that in dissolving it grows like a father whereas in deficiency it is lacking; and in my humble opinion Tur's words are compelling, for if it were forbidden treifah, likewise laketah through dissolving — for all that dissolved is deficiency; if so the fact that at the beginning of chapter Elu Treifot Ulla counted eight types of treifot and did not count laketah — to exclude laketah that adheres in the kulya; and it is difficult, for he counted the category of deficiency; if so deficiency of the kulya that is treifah, and all the more so laketah in dissolving which is deficiency that the physician scrapes — and Rashi counted "its leg was cut" and meeting of sinews in the category of deficiency, and all the more so deficiency of the kulya; and what does it exclude? There is an aleph-vav in "deficient" — deficient is kosher, only laketah; if so it is impossible to include it in the category of deficiency, for deficient is kosher, and especially if we say stam adhesion said laketah in the kulya is treifah, and people of the west say until it reaches the place of the charitz, but stam adhesion said even if it did not reach the place of the charitz (and see what I wrote above siman 29); and it is difficult from the Mishnah: one cuts from spleen and kidneys and you have no deficiency from what one cuts — a piece is cut and left in the body; and likewise cut is permitted and laketah deals with dissolving and forbidden — and behold Tur's and Taz's words are clear and compelling. However one may say on the other side per Maharshal: at first glance it is difficult, for the beginning of chapter Elu Treifot implies the halachah is like Ulla who counted eight types excluding this adhesion; and here we hold like Rabbi Shimon; and see what I wrote above siman 29 for Rambam: aleph-vav in adhesion — to exclude adhesion that does not need "until the place of the charitz"; if so it comes to exclude that it is not in the category of deficiency, for deficient is kosher; and this is proven from the Mishnah in Behemah HaMaksheh. But per what we hold that we require the place of the charitz — if so resolve the difficulty of that Mishnah cutting from spleen and kidneys, and one may well say it is in the category of deficiency, and there is no difficulty of halachah against halachah; and Maharshal has support from this.`,
    '3#_': `That its flesh becomes. Taz brought in s.k. 4 in name of Maharshal: internal deficiency in the kulya is kosher — only requires study, since it reaches the charitz internal deficiency is not applicable; and I did not understand, for we say kosher in lung, disqualifies in kulya — meaning putrefaction per Rashi: tin-roof, Heaven forbid there should be putrefaction passing through a membrane — there is no greater laketah than this; and it must be putrefaction only under the membrane and from it the substance of tin-roof; and behold internal deficiency; and it is difficult; and in such a case even in the kulya kosher. And there is no difference between lung and kulya; unless where it teaches punctured like lung, and as stated regarding spleen — there internal deficiency is not called deficiency; but where it teaches stam laketah — ultimately there is laketah and treifah, and it is clear.`,
    '4#_': `Until the loven — Shach's view and most acharonim: laketah must be in the loven itself. And one should understand per what the Mechaber wrote in seif 3 that laketah in the loven alone is treifah; if so, why was "laketah in the kulya" stated — it should have taught only that if there is laketah in the loven, necessarily if the loven alone was afflicted it is treifah without the entire kulya; and if the loven was not afflicted, even laketah in the entire kulya is kosher. Do not say this difficulty also applies to liver with removal of an olive in the place of gall, etc. — there in liver it was never stated at all, only mentioned in the Mishnah; and one view holds no olive is required at all, and the other teaches "liver removed and an olive remained" — kosher; how could it teach and resolve? And in flaying there is apparently difficulty why it teaches "flayed" meaning all the skin peeled, and not only skin peeled from the place of the spine is treifah — in any case if it exists there in truth it is kosher, and if not it is treifah in them. But one may say: flaying requires the entire spine be covered in skin without a bare place, but peeling only from the spine requires the entire spine be bare; half covered half exposed is kosher, unlike flaying where all must be covered — if not, treifah; therefore there is a resolution; but here regarding loven it is difficult; and it is strained to say adhesion does not require laketah in the loven, and properly it says laketah in the kulya; and people of the west who disagree hold we require the place of the charitz in truth — they disagree; and it is strained; and it appears more that in "laketah in the kulya," if affliction spread in part of the place of the loven — treifah, for the entire kulya joins and it is majority afflicted, unlike laketah in the loven alone where we require majority of the loven — minority is nullified to majority; and follow the majority; requires study. Or distinguish: in "laketah in the kulya" and it was lost and we do not know if affliction spread in the place of the loven — treifah as explained in responsum of Maharshadam siman 35 and Rivan's words — see there; but if there is only laketah in the loven — certainty is required that there was affliction there, and from doubt we do not declare treifah; we leave it in presumption of kosher status; requires study.`,
    '5#_': `Sword-blow, etc. They wondered: behold Rama permits in cut; Bach and Taz explain it deals with split in two; and Shach already rejected and wrote well: if split in two, whoever declares treifah declares treifah even without a sword, even by illness; and the reason they learned from Job "He splits my kidneys" — the Satan did not stab him with a sword. And Shach explained with force — this too Pri Chadash rejected; the law is with him, for who can measure and weigh whether it was done with force or not, and not all forces are equal when the matter is entrusted to each person. But the matter is simple: certainly if something was cut, even by sword, it does not pain so much; circumcision proves — they circumcise the foreskin with a knife and it heals easily; but if one makes wounds in flesh by striking with a sword — that generates heat and poison inside and it is danger — and this is sword-blow; and see Avodah Zarah 28b Tosafot entry "a blow on the hand" who wrote: illness not from sword-blow, but from sword-blow we say everywhere one desecrates Shabbat as they said per his view, etc.; and they explain sword-blow as wounds from sword-blow — see there; and in poskim and Mordechai you will find; and this is what Rama intended. And one may learn for Maharshach and his supporters who hold split in two is treifah and derive from Job. Difficulty: Ulla does not hold this adhesion; how is R' Yochanan's statement resolved where R' Shimon answered the rabbis from "He splits my kidneys" — how is it established for him that it is kosher, and all the more so per what Tosafot wrote on daf 54 that R' Yochanan saw adhesion and R' Yochanan himself has this statement where colleagues answered R' Yochanan from this verse — there is resolution — see there.`,
    '6#_': `Until the loven — Shach in s.k. 8 and more acharonim brought the view of many authors that in sword-blow we require until the place of the charitz, but in putrefaction in laketah any amount is treifah. And I did not understand, for it appears from the Gemara people of the west that they were concerned until the charitz, but adhesion holds even if it did not reach the charitz; and if you say it deals in sword-blow — how is the Mishnah "one cuts from spleen and kidneys" resolved, and why the first answer in the Gemara that in truth it is treifah; and if so, why did the Gemara challenge R' Elazar and not adhesion; or adhesion who said laketah — say only in putrefaction; and it is proven that here too we require until the charitz; and so is correct.`,
    '7#_': `If blood was found — its law is like water. And Maharshal was stringent; later authors wrote in name of Orach Chaim the reason: the kulya in its substance is blood, etc.; per his view that in laketah we do not require the place of the loven — fine that he wrote regarding blood found that this is not laketah for such is its nature; but per what we hold that laketah requires the place of the charitz — it must be that the place of the loven too turned to blood; in this "it is entirely blood" does not apply, for it is milk as known in puncturing for all; therefore requires study. Also it appears to me that we permit when the measure of the kulya remains only on account of laketah in this we say the nature of the kulya is thus; but when there is no measure of kulya like a bean or grape — who will help that it is blood; and if we say it turned to flesh — likewise in liver; behold we say regarding two olive-volumes that turned to blood — treifah; and now the entire liver is blood — we do not say it will return and turn to liver flesh; how can we say so in the kulya? And all the more it challenges Rama above regarding liver where he holds it is laketah even though the two olive-volumes did not melt — we say their end is melting that is laketah; and here in the kulya he does not hold laketah at all. And one cannot say "we do not compare this to that" in treifot when this was not stated in Gemara and early poskim until it is said one may not learn one from the other. And it appears to me it is not blood definitively, only the water's appearance is like blood; and this is why he permits, for there is no laketah here, for the substance of the kulya is blood, and if some drops of blood mix in the water immediately the water will have the appearance of blood completely until every viewer judges it is entirely blood; therefore there is no laketah, and all difficulties are resolved; but actual blood is laketah like in liver and one should be stringent.`,
    '8#_': `And how can it return to kosher status — and Maharshal cried out "what of it"; and see what acharonim wrote, especially Taz; and I brought proof per what Maharshal wrote that treifah can become pregnant but cannot give birth; if so the Gemara's forcing regarding fetus of treifah — R' Eliezer says do not offer and R' Yochanan says offer — according to the one who says treifah does not bear, what do they argue; and there is no need for both to hold treifah does not bear; and the Gemara is established in pregnancy and finally treifah, and in the fetus it leans on its mother. We challenged the first case: in this scenario where the kulya shrank and it is treifah and became pregnant — treifah becomes pregnant and before its days to give birth it healed when the kulya was removed and behold it is kosher and able to give birth; at least when it became pregnant the animal was treifah, and R' Eliezer and R' Yochanan argue whether each causes or is permitted or forbidden; no need to establish in another dispute. However this may be rejected: what Maharshal wrote that it can become pregnant but not give birth means it cannot bear living offspring from permanent seed, only bear a non-viable birth; therefore if it gives birth and the offspring lives, that is a sign it is not treifah; but to give birth alone Maharshal concedes, for in "became pregnant and afterward became treifah" the Gemara says it bears; if so my proof falls, for when it became pregnant it was treifah — it had no ability to become pregnant with living offspring that it bore afterward — what of it that it returned to kosher status; nevertheless Tosafot do not hold like Maharshal's view in Chullin 11b that we follow majority from false witnesses, etc. — Tosafot challenged: bind him twelve months, if he lives it is not treifah; what is the difficulty, since we do not follow majority and we are concerned for all doubts — perhaps at the time of testimony the murderer's kulya shrank and he was treifah and witnesses are exempt, and what shrank afterward until the kulya was removed and returned to kosher status and he lived even a hundred years, and either way witnesses at time of testimony are exempt; and similarly one may infer in what Tosafot challenged in Zevachim 101a: perhaps it was treifah at the time of the Flood; it lived many years — one may say it returned to kosher status as above; only this is not such a proof, for Tosafot wrote in 101a that treifot need not be visible, and therefore Maharshal's law almost does not exist in reality; but from my proof it is difficult — examine. And for practical law per Shulchan Aruch: even in spleen removed by illness it is kosher — illness where it shrank, for spleen is not treifah through shrinking and there is no measure for its size; but if removed by illness where it melted — treifah, for initially it melted in the supports, i.e. flesh rotted that the physician scrapes, and it is treifah; how can it return to kosher status when removed; therefore in truth treifah; and Shulchan Aruch relied on the kulya — examine.`,
    '9#_': `Created, etc. kosher. Rambam wrote in chapter 8 of Hilchot Shechitah law 25: created with one kulya — kosher; and I wonder, for in Bechorot 39 a Baraisa teaches: "and the two kidneys" — not one who has one kulya and not one who has three — to disqualify for the altar; and another Baraisa teaches: "you shall remove them" — to include one who has one kulya — permitted; and the Gemara challenges them and resolves: they disagree whether a creature is created with one; one Sage holds there is a creature created with one, and one holds there is not; and certainly it is deficient; and the one who permits holds there is a creature with one created with one and there is no deficiency and kosher; and R' Yochanan said: all agree there is no creature with one, only here it lacked before slaughter, etc.; in any case we hear from this Baraisa "two kidneys" and not one with one kulya or three — there is no creature with one; and Rambam in chapter 2 of Hilchot Issurei Mizbeach copied this Baraisa, and so Rif brought it; therefore he must hold there is no creature with one; how did he write in his book of Shechitah that created with one kulya is kosher — it does not exist in reality; requires study at first glance. And what one may say: Rambam and poskim explain opposite Rashi's explanation of this Baraisa "and the two kidneys" — it appears to him there is a creature with one; therefore the Torah specified two kidneys, certainly excluding one and three, and two specifically even if created lacking, for the similarity of three is as explained there in the Gemara that we require one similar to three; if so, how can the other Baraisa say "you shall remove them" includes one with one kulya — behold in that verse "two kidneys" was excluded; why need a verse to include deficiency afterward — all the more so when created lacking it is disqualified, all the more so when removed; and it is simple; therefore "you shall remove them" is for another exposition; but the other Baraisa holds there is no creature with one; if so, why "two kidneys" to exclude one, for it does not exist in reality; and for deficiency there is no need, for we require similarity to three; and see Rashi there entry "Mar disqualified," etc., what Rashi wrote in name of his teacher and Rashi strained in the text — and for us it is unnecessary. And therefore "two kidneys" does not come to exclude one; only "one who has three"; though not in the plain meaning of the language, nevertheless it was strained and established; and if so we expound "you shall remove them" to include one with one kulya even if lacking, for there is no internal deficiency; and one may say: since we hold the Baraisa "two kidneys," etc., as Rambam wrote, we also hold there is a creature with one. And Pri Chadash end of siman 41 challenged: "every excess is considered as removed" is only where there is examination; why is one with three kidneys disqualified for the Most High, for there is no deficiency here until the name of imperfection applies; and the resolution: Rashba and supporters as stated there — reason disqualified due to perfect; but in conclusion we expound "two kidneys," etc., to exclude one created without a kulya — not from reason of deficiency, for in the sugya one created without a kulya is not called deficient, only by scriptural decree; and likewise for one with three — by scriptural decree, not from deficiency; and it is correct. And thereby Tosafot Chullin 11b are understood: we follow majority from red heifer lest it was treifah and the Torah calls it a sin-offering; and the Posek challenged: since a cow is two years old and treifah does not live more than twelve months; if so it has presumption; and one must say he holds treifah lives, as Tosafot wrote there entry "and perhaps," etc.; if so what is the difficulty for the one who says treifah does not live — from where do we learn we follow majority; and what is the difficulty — behold we are concerned for the minority; if so, perhaps this cow has only one kulya or three, and it is disqualified for the altar, and even for the cow disqualified, for the Torah calls it a sin-offering, and it is not treifah; and you can revive it; and that the Gemara stated in the language "treifah" means it holds treifah lives, but in truth the law is clear to all. And one must say Tosafot explain like Rashi above, not like Rambam; and therefore per what we ruled like the Baraisa "two kidneys," there is no reality of an animal lacking kidneys from creation, and there is no concern here, only lest it lacked afterward by illness similarly; and on this we follow presumption, for it was certainly born with two kidneys. And per what we said "perfect" was excluded only by scriptural decree; and in Tosafot entry "sin-offering," etc., they wrote it is not certain treifah is disqualified because the Torah calls it a sin-offering, for that refers only to it; and in three kidneys it applies; and resolve the difficulty; examine.`,
    '10#_': `There are no treifot in the kulya of fowl. The more correct and simple reason: there is no charitz in it, and all treifot depend on the charitz — this contradicts those who hold in putrefaction and such we do not require charitz; if so, it should disqualify in fowl too; and similarly from shrunken kulya that charitz must also shrink proportionally — it should disqualify in fowl; and see similarly; this is not so, for the measure of kulya in a thin animal is a bean — likewise the measure of charitz in a thin animal is the length of a bean along the length of the kulya; if it shrank, the charitz too shrank below its proper measure — therefore treifah; and this does not apply in fowl, for there is no charitz. And Agur gave the reason that they are enclosed and there is no concern intestines will damage — as those who hold in putrefaction we require charitz. And what was challenged on him: from where do we know kidney treifah is from damage to intestines — it appears he holds like Rashi at the beginning of chapter Behemah HaMaksheh explains: therefore spleen and kidneys were stated in the Mishnah — not that they are treifah in them, meaning life does not depend on them to permit or forbid; and spleen is treifah in the supports because it damages the rumen; and Agur holds likewise kidneys — from where; and examine. And if challenged: how could the beginning of chapter Elu Treifot count laketah in the kulya among treifot — behold treifot from intestines as Tosafot wrote, therefore it counted deficiency of the skull; see above siman 29 what I wrote there.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Or cut. Baer Heitev: likewise deficient; and see Taz who wrote Maharshal disagrees with Tur and is stringent in deficiency, and Taz rejected Maharshal's reasoning and rules like Tur to be lenient; and so Pri Chadash. And see responsum Shemen Rokeach siman 35 who brought proof for Maharshal's view and concluded that without great financial loss one should be stringent — see there; and see responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 14 what he wrote. And what Baer Heitev further wrote: specifically by needle, etc. — see responsum Toldot Yitzchak there who brought in name of Sefer Be'er Avraham to be lenient even in puncture by illness; and he challenged him and elaborated to strengthen Orach Chaim's words — Heaven forbid to be lenient; and he wrote: specifically if it reached the place of the charitz; but otherwise one may be lenient — see there; and it appears he means even if the puncture is open outside one may be lenient; but if the puncture is only from inside, in putrefaction too it is kosher if it did not reach the loven per Maharshal whom Baer Heitev brought below s.k. 4; and I wrote there that this is the essence, because internal deficiency is not called deficiency — we say likewise regarding the kulya.`,
    '2#_': `The loven. Baer Heitev; and Sefer Bnei Chayei wrote: a kulya where they saw laketah and the examiner did not manage to examine whether laketah reached the charitz — Maharshadam Yoreh De'ah siman 38 and Maharshach part 1 siman 77 ruled the animal treifah; however the vessels in which this meat was cooked may be permitted — see there the reason; see Pri Megadim whose view is to forbid the vessels even after the fact — see there; and see responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 11 on this matter.`,
    '3#_': `That they reach. Baer Heitev: most poskim disagree except Pri Chadash; and see Kol Ephraim who also tends to be lenient; and in responsum Kenesset Yechezkel siman 25 he brings in name of Or Zaru'a, author of Panim Me'irot, who proved like Shulchan Aruch's view — that laketah and putrefaction are one matter; and therefore even in putrefaction kosher without reaching the charitz; but he challenged him and raised that in putrefaction obviously treifah even without reaching the loven; however in murky and rotting water, one whose view is broad may disagree with all acharonim; I am obligated not to depart from acharonim to forbid even in murky and rotting that did not reach the charitz — see there; and see responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 9 who brought in name of Sefer Be'er Avraham who contradicted Kenesset Yechezkel and proved like Pri Megadim, even in putrefaction kosher without reaching the charitz; but he elaborated to disagree and raised like Kenesset Yechezkel that one may not depart from acharonim to forbid even without double doubt in putrefaction and murky water even if they did not reach the charitz; and he elaborated that Rama's view is also thus; and what he wrote sword-blow's law is like laketah of the kulya — because he holds in dissolving laketah we require reaching the place of the charitz; for what they said we require affliction reach the charitz deals with dissolving laketah; but in putrefaction and murky water he too concedes treifah even without reaching the charitz; and therefore he did not place this hagahah at the end — see there [and there at the beginning of siman 14 he wrote one may rely on this to permit dissolving not in the place of the loven]. And he further wrote what Baer Heitev wrote: two testified they saw the kulya without putrefaction and two say, etc. — Pri Megadim brought this in name of Rashdam siman 38 and concludes: however if all say there was putrefaction, only two say it reached the loven and two say it did not — it is certainly treifah, for there is double doubt to forbid, etc. — see there. And behold this is simple; and in my humble opinion it is preferable to say: even if two say there was no putrefaction at all but two say there was and it reached the loven — also treifah rabbinically, for every two against two is a rabbinic doubt, and from rabbinic law we forbid a Torah prohibition; and see responsum Chatam Sofer part 2.`,
    '4#_': `Clear water. [See responsum Chatam Sofer siman 47: even if they are salty one may not forbid — see there.]`,
    '5#_': `And such is the custom. See responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 12 who wrote: in this where it was like appearance of honey — forbid only if certainly it reached the place of the charitz; but if there was doubt whether it reached the place of the charitz — permit — see there.`,
    '6#_': `Except in the loven. See Kol Ephraim who wrote: even if the kulya was afflicted and reached the place of the loven, and in the loven somewhat — treifah; nevertheless if affliction is only in the loven, majority of the loven is required. Also if we saw laketah and did not manage to examine the loven until it was lost — treifah (as above in name of Rashdam and Maharshach); but if there is only laketah in the loven — certainty is required that there was affliction there; but from doubt we do not declare treifah; and see responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 10 who wrote his words are not compelling and he raised for practical law there is no distinction, and even if only the loven was afflicted somewhat — treifah; but what Kol Ephraim wrote that from doubt we do not declare treifah — his law is true, and he added a commendable reason — see there.`,
    '7#_': `And how do we know. See responsum Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 62: a matter where one kidney was found very large and the second shrank very much and they could not determine whether it was from creation or not — he answered to permit, for since the second grew not from illness, certainly from creation it was thus; therefore the second too shrank from creation at the beginning; great halachic authorities agreed — see there.`,
    '8#_': `And how can it return to kosher status. See Baer Heitev s.k. 7; and see responsum Yeri'ot HaOhel siman 25 who elaborated to bring proof for Maharshal to permit in this; nevertheless he concludes he is afraid to decide, especially to be lenient; but in place of great loss and the like one may rely and permit — see there; and see She'ilat Yaavetz part 2 siman 107 whose view is to forbid even in great loss — see there [and see responsum Mishkenot Yaakov siman 20 what he wrote, and he concludes the lenient did not lose and has support in Rashi's words Bechorot 39 — see there; and see what I wrote below siman 81 seif 2 regarding milk from such an animal — see there].`,
    '9#_': `One must check. See responsum Toldot Yitzchak siman 13 who wrote: if they did not manage to examine their pouch until it was lost — treifah from doubt — see there; and per responsum Yeri'ot HaOhel that I brought above, it appears one may be lenient; see beginning of Pri Megadim on Yoreh De'ah, general rules of Orach Chaim ot 16.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `Or cut even until the place of the charitz. Baer Heitev; and in Machatzit HaShekel he elaborated and raised: puncture by needle even without deficiency, if it reached the loven and the flesh is not healthy — be stringent even without double doubt; and if it reached the loven and there is no deficiency and the flesh is healthy and good and there is only the puncture — permit with double doubt if the puncture did not reach the loven and there is no deficiency and the flesh has a flaw and also the flaw is not in the loven — permit; all this when it is known the puncture was by needle. But if it cannot be clarified that it was by needle and there is no flaw in the flesh — if there is no deficiency, even if it reached the loven — permit — see there; and see Machatzit HaShekel in name of Maharash Halevi: needle found in kidneys reaching the loven, the needle recognizable as from one tree that the shepherd takes with a little iron on it like a needle; the thick end found outside the animal's skin and the thin end inside — there was great mixture in animals and in kidneys; he answered widespread law: the kulya was punctured — kosher. And one may challenge: since it passed through the esophagus, etc. — this does not exist, for it is recognizable this needle entered from the skin to the kidneys, as a shepherd of sheep strikes near the kidneys; it is recognizable its thick end is outside, etc. — see there.`,
    '2#_': `And it is when they reach. Baer Heitev; and see Pri Megadim part 2 siman 108 whose view is like Pri Chadash; and so there siman 120; and Or Zaru'a in Turei Shalom disagrees with Pri Chadash on this; and so Kenesset Yechezkel siman 105 — see there. And see Pri Megadim there on a blister in the kulya whose appearance is like saffron: even though one may say per Rif and Rambam all that are not thick and murky are in the category of clear — nevertheless one may not rely to permit except when they reached the place of the charitz — see there; and see in my books on treifot s.k. 5 and siman 41 where I elaborated on this.`,
    '3#_': `Only they melted and treifah. Baer Heitev: what he wrote in name of Shevut Yaakov; and abbreviated understandably — see Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 62: an animal where one kidney was found very large and the second shrank very much and they could not determine whether from creation — I ruled to permit, for since the first grew not from illness, certainly from creation; therefore the second too shrank from creation at the beginning; great halachic authorities agreed and it came out permitted — see there; and there he wrote explicitly Shach's words siman 44 s.k. 15.`,
    '4#_': `And any change of appearance. Baer Heitev in name of Pri Chadash; and Machatzit HaShekel wrote in name of Ohel Yosef siman 14: a kulya whose appearance changed — they practiced in Saloniki to boil it; if it returned to its appearance — kosher, for sometimes blackening in the kulya is from fatigue and this is not laketah — see there that his view is change of appearance does not disqualify unless it reached the charitz, as Maharshal wrote, unlike Taz. And if a Jew said he saw a black spot on the kulya and a non-Jew took it — permit from double doubt per what Chatam Sofer wrote regarding shrunken kulya — see there.`,
    '5#_': `A kulya that reddened. See Turei Moshe in name of Turei Shalom: reddening of the loven — there is no greater change of appearance than this; and as his words are explained in responsum of Mahari ibn Machir siman 79: since reddening of the loven from a blow becomes laketah, etc. — see there; and Machatzit HaShekel brought it from responsum of Chatam Sofer (which had not yet been printed).`,
  },
};
